    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(13))  # C0..C12\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(5, 5), (88, 61), (51, 61), (30, 0), (10, 83)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(22, 9), (85, 92), (74, 63), (44, 50), (63, 28), (87, 68), (4, 29), (80, 48), (37, 74), (72, 49), (15, 30), (63, 60), (11, 26)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [204.0, 175.0, 135.0, 295.0, 280.0]\n    customer_demands = [45.0, 56.0, 55.0, 14.0, 39.0, 50.0, 19.0, 13.0, 12.0, 22.0, 29.0, 55.0, 46.0]\n    fixed_costs = [337.0, 268.0, 299.0, 257.0, 262.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.86  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Decision on Which Warehouses to Open and How to Serve Our Customers\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which of our potential warehouse sites to open and how to route orders from those sites to our customer locations. The objective is straightforward: meet every customer\u2019s demand while keeping our total cost as low as possible. Total cost includes what we spend to open and operate the selected warehouses and what we pay to move product from those warehouses to each customer.\n\nWe have five candidate warehouse sites and thirteen customer destinations. Shipping costs will depend on how far each warehouse is from each customer and how much we ship. Each warehouse can only handle so much volume, and some locations will be cheaper to open than others. The right answer balances these trade-offs\u2014opening enough capacity in the right places to cover all demand, while avoiding unnecessary fixed costs and long, expensive delivery routes.\n\nAvailable inputs\n- Candidate warehouse locations (map coordinates): Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (5, 5)\n  - F1: (88, 61)\n  - F2: (51, 61)\n  - F3: (30, 0)\n  - F4: (10, 83)\n- Customer locations (map coordinates): Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (22, 9)\n  - C1: (85, 92)\n  - C2: (74, 63)\n  - C3: (44, 50)\n  - C4: (63, 28)\n  - C5: (87, 68)\n  - C6: (4, 29)\n  - C7: (80, 48)\n  - C8: (37, 74)\n  - C9: (72, 49)\n  - C10: (15, 30)\n  - C11: (63, 60)\n  - C12: (11, 26)\n- One-time opening costs for each candidate site: [337.0, 268.0, 299.0, 257.0, 262.0]\n- Maximum throughput each site can support: [204.0, 175.0, 135.0, 295.0, 280.0]\n- Demand that must be fulfilled for each customer: [45.0, 56.0, 55.0, 14.0, 39.0, 50.0, 19.0, 13.0, 12.0, 22.0, 29.0, 55.0, 46.0]\n- Transport rate applied to distance and shipped volume: 0.86\n\nBusiness rules and expectations\n- A warehouse must be opened before it can ship any product.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied; no partial or missed orders.\n- No warehouse may ship more than its available capacity.\n- Shipping costs rise with both the distance traveled and the quantity shipped, so closer assignments are generally more economical when capacity allows.\n- We will prioritize the combination of sites and shipment plans that delivers all orders at the lowest total cost, considering both the fixed costs to open facilities and the variable costs to move product.\n\nDecision to make\n- Which warehouse sites should we open?\n- How should we allocate each customer\u2019s orders across the opened sites to stay within capacity limits while minimizing total cost?\n\nWhat success looks like\n- A recommended set of warehouses to open, with a clear rationale tied to cost and coverage.\n- An order allocation plan showing which customers are served from which sites, demonstrating that all demand is met and no capacity limits are exceeded.\n- An estimate of total cost under the recommended plan, broken down into opening and shipping components, along with a comparison to plausible alternatives if helpful.\n\nOnce we align on the decision, we can proceed to implementation planning and timeline, including any startup activities required for the selected sites and communication to affected customers.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse Network Decision\u2014Which Sites to Open and How to Serve Our Customers\n\nWe need to decide which of our candidate warehouses to open and how to route shipments so we meet all customer demand at the lowest overall cost. The five candidate warehouse locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and our thirteen customer locations are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Opening any warehouse comes with a one-time expense captured in {FIXED_COSTS}. Each warehouse has a limit on how much it can handle, summarized in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer has a demand that must be fully satisfied, shown in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Shipping costs depend on how far each warehouse is from each customer and how much we ship; our transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nThe decision in front of us is twofold:\n- Which warehouses should we open?\n- How should we allocate shipments from the open warehouses to cover all customer demand while staying within each site\u2019s capacity?\n\nA few practical guardrails apply. A warehouse can only ship if it\u2019s opened. All customer demand must be fully met. And shipping costs will rise the farther product needs to travel and the more units we move. The trade-off is straightforward: opening more warehouses can shorten delivery distances and reduce variable shipping costs, but it increases fixed costs. Opening fewer warehouses saves on fixed costs but may push up shipping costs or strain capacity at the sites we do open.\n\nOur goal is to minimize total cost by balancing these factors\u2014fixed opening costs and distance-driven shipping costs\u2014while ensuring every customer is covered and no warehouse exceeds its capacity. The outcome we\u2019re targeting is a clear network plan: the subset of warehouses to open and an allocation of shipments from those sites to each customer that meets demand and keeps costs down."
